      "seed": 1447818397,
      "version": 125,
      "versionNonce": 1973882845,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793839609295,
      "created": 1793839309336,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Functional Requirements\n- Add usage\n- Remove usage\n- Peek usage, with an indicator of over budget\n\nNon-functional Requirement\n- Scale: 10B users * 100/user/day = 1T req/day \n- 10 ^12 / 10 ^5 = 10 Million req/sec\n- 10^7 / 10^4 - 1000 servers\n- Highly Available & eventually consistent  < 10s\n- Low latency Peek at backend < 200ms\n—------stretch goal—---------\n- Durability for adding/remove usage api\n- Able to handle peak scale which 10T req/day\n\n\nOut of Scope\n- Managing service degradation\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Functional Requirements\n- Add usage\n- Remove usage\n- Peek usage, with an indicator of over budget\n\nNon-functional Requirement\n- Scale: 10B users * 100/user/day = 1T req/day \n- 10 ^12 / 10 ^5 = 10 Million req/sec\n- 10^7 / 10^4 - 1000 servers\n- Highly Available & eventually consistent  < 10s\n- Low latency Peek at backend < 200ms\n—------stretch goal—---------\n- Durability for adding/remove usage api\n- Able to handle peak scale which 10T req/day\n\n\nOut of Scope\n- Managing service degradation\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ooLE5xHP4v8nxaUa9zzaR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -250,
      "y": 1167.5,
      "width": 187.36001586914062,
      "height": 200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2C",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 914932371,
      "version": 33,
      "versionNonce": 1385343293,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793839416002,
      "created": 1793839396996,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Core Entities\n- Storage\n- StorageMetadata\n- User\n\nAPIs\n- \n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Core Entities\n- Storage\n- StorageMetadata\n- User\n\nAPIs\n- \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "WFqq0JLewXvQbB8HB3LWX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -263.96484375,
      "y": 359.921875,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2D",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2073088531,
      "version": 82,
      "versionNonce": 735089299,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793839477924,
      "created": 1793839470118,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Google One (G1) is a paid-subscription service with the original anchor in\nStorage. The way storage accounting works in Google is that each individual user\nstarts with a default 15GB of storage and usages across all Google products\n(Gmail, Drive, Photos, Backups) count against this quota. If a user goes above\nthe quota, they experience service degradation across Google surfaces, like their\nGoogle Drive going into a read-only mode, Gmail stops delivering emails, etc. The\nway a user recovers from this state is by either purchasing additional storage\n(from G1) or deleting existing data.\n\nThe goal is to design a system which can do storage accounting (available quota\nand usage) for all Google users.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Google One (G1) is a paid-subscription service with the original anchor in Storage. The way storage accounting works in Google is that each individual user starts with a default 15GB of storage and usages across all Google products (Gmail, Drive, Photos, Backups) count against this quota. If a user goes above the quota, they experience service degradation across Google surfaces, like their Google Drive going into a read-only mode, Gmail stops delivering emails, etc. The way a user recovers from this state is by either purchasing additional storage (from G1) or deleting existing data.\n\nThe goal is to design a system which can do storage accounting (available quota and usage) for all Google users.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "bEvddd0vtS8MHUIXYaBAd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 298.1171875,
      "y": 892.4765625,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2E",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1801873565,
      "version": 1746,
      "versionNonce": 1974273555,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793924199740,
      "created": 1793920204317,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Deep Dives:\n1. Average Case: we have 10M req/sec. However, for an average user it's only\n100/user/day i.e. 1 request every 15 minutes. So this does not need any special\nhandling\n2. Burst Case Write Path: However, for the burst cases, we can end up with\n1000s of request/second coming through distributed servers, and getting in a\nrace condition on a single record in the StorageMetadata table\n    2.1 A better option is to make the requests stateful, using zookeeper and\nensuring the same user's request land on the same Storage Write server every\ntime.\n    2.2 To maintain the consumer side SLA, we can even introduce Kafka for\nwrites plus faster acknowledgement, maintaining the order of events by\npartitioning Kafka by userId. \n    2.3 Now the Storage write server acts as a producer. We can introduce 2\nconsumers: 1) the consumer can be reading from the kafka partition, and\nsequentially adding events to the Storage table as a simple batch with writes\nevery 1s, and 2) the second consumer would read the same kafka entries, and be\nbatching the StorageMetadata updates for 1s batches of compressed entry, to\nreduce the write burden on that table, without breaching the SLA of 10s\neventual consistency.\n    2.4 When the consumer makes an entry about the availableStorage in the\nStorageMetadata it also updates the lastAppliedOffset that it read from kafka.\nIn the case when the Consumer dies, the new consumer picks back up from this\noffset without losing any updates.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Deep Dives:\n1. Average Case: we have 10M req/sec. However, for an average user it's only 100/user/day i.e. 1 request every 15 minutes. So this does not need any special handling\n2. Burst Case Write Path: However, for the burst cases, we can end up with 1000s of request/second coming through distributed servers, and getting in a race condition on a single record in the StorageMetadata table\n    2.1 A better option is to make the requests stateful, using zookeeper and ensuring the same user's request land on the same Storage Write server every time.\n    2.2 To maintain the consumer side SLA, we can even introduce Kafka for writes plus faster acknowledgement, maintaining the order of events by partitioning Kafka by userId. \n    2.3 Now the Storage write server acts as a producer. We can introduce 2 consumers: 1) the consumer can be reading from the kafka partition, and sequentially adding events to the Storage table as a simple batch with writes every 1s, and 2) the second consumer would read the same kafka entries, and be batching the StorageMetadata updates for 1s batches of compressed entry, to reduce the write burden on that table, without breaching the SLA of 10s eventual consistency.\n    2.4 When the consumer makes an entry about the availableStorage in the StorageMetadata it also updates the lastAppliedOffset that it read from kafka. In the case when the Consumer dies, the new consumer picks back up from this offset without losing any updates.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
